.TH CHTTPSVR_ROUTER_ON 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_router_on \- register a buffered handler on a sub-router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_router_on(chttpsvr_router *router,
                                  chttp_method_t method,
                                  const char *pattern,
                                  chttpsvr_handler_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function works like chttpsvr_register_handler, but it registers
the route on a sub-router.  Every rule that chttpsvr_register_handler
describes applies here too.  Those rules cover the routing, the slash
at the start, the slash at the end, two slashes together, the
characters of a parameter name, a parameter name that appears twice,
and the thread safety.  This function also supports CHTTP_ANY as the
method argument.

This function first checks that nothing destroyed the server that owns
router.  That check also covers a concurrent destroy from another
thread.  A call that races a chttpsvr_destroy of the owning server
returns ccol_invalid_args, and touches no freed memory.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when router, pattern or fn is NULL; when pattern
                  does not start with '/'; when pattern has two
                  slashes together or a slash at the end; when a
                  {name} segment has a character outside
                  [A-Za-z0-9_]; when the same {name} appears more than
                  once; or when something destroyed the server that
                  owns router.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
